Deliberately hand-written
-------------------------

Article 2.7 requires a mature cross-platform library wherever one exists. These
are the recorded exceptions, each with its reason.

  piricad/command/task.hpp
      The C++20 coroutine Task<T>. piricad.md §7.1 makes writing it the decision:
      "cppcoro (MIT, bakımı yavaş) veya kendi minimal Task<T> tipinizi yazın —
      komut altyapısı için 300 satır yeterlidir ve tam kontrol sağlar.
      Öneri: kendiniz yazın."

  piricad/core/text.hpp — turkish_upper
      Seven cased pairs (i/İ, ı/I, ç, ğ, ö, ş, ü) as an exact table. ICU is the
      right answer for collation, normalisation and search, and casing moves onto
      it when those land; pulling in ~30 MB of ICU for seven pairs is not the
      trade today. The table is exact for its domain and is tested.

  piricad/app/flow_layout.hpp — FlowLayout
      Qt ships no wrapping layout as API. The reflowing layout every Qt
      application needs is published as an example (Qt Widgets "Flow Layout"),
      not as a QFlowLayout class, so there is nothing to link against and no
      third-party package that is more mature than the documented pattern
      itself. ~110 lines implementing QLayout::heightForWidth. Replaced the day
      Qt promotes it to API.

  piricad/core/json.hpp, tests/support/microtest.hpp, piricad/core/log.hpp
      Standing in for nlohmann/json, doctest and spdlog until the section above
      is genuinely wired. These are not the decision — the decision is the SHA —
      they are what the code calls in the meantime, and they are listed here so
      that "what does PiriCAD actually contain" has one honest answer.
      core/log.hpp additionally carries the CLAUDE.md Article 8.4 waiver.

  the command bus, registry, parser, journal, document model
      No library provides these. They are the product.

